00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:00.340
So GKE On-Prem-- first of
all, hopefully the name

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.090
describes it all.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:02.620
It's Google's Kubernetes engine.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:05.590
We've taken Kubernetes,
open-source Kubernetes.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.480
We've built it in
the opinionated way

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.230
that we, Google, want to
run Kubernetes and host that

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.820
for our customers, for our
users of Google Cloud who want

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.459
a curated hosted experience.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:20.709
Now that's not the only way
you can use and run Kubernetes.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.110
There's lots of ways you
can use and run Kubernetes.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.230
There's over 100
distributions of Kubernetes.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.150
You can take it and
build it yourself.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:28.780
I have.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.992
It's fun.

00:04:29.992 --> 00:04:31.450
But sometimes you
want someone else

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:36.310
to worry about managing
that, running that data

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.430
center, that hardware, those
VMs, securing the entire stack,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.190
providing the updates, the
security, the maintenance

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.060
over time.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:47.230
And that's what GKE is, Google's
hosted Kubernetes engine.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.510
And of course our competitors
have their own offerings.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:53.170
What we're doing here
is, we're literally

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.710
taking the same bits, the
exact same bits that we use,

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.750
and we run inside
of Google's data

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.740
centers to provide GKE
Google's hosted version.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.260
But we're going to do that
on prem in a data center

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:10.380
wherever it is that
you want to run GKE.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.540
The asterisk here is
part of the safe harbor.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.350
I'm required to tell you this
is now an early access program.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.360
And I have a URL for
you at the very end

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.000
where you can go
and sign up to have

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.170
early access to this
technology and these features,

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.240
this extension of
Google Cloud itself.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:27.980
Now what is it?

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:31.790
Fundamentally this is, I
think, all in one slide

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:34.460
this is just about it all.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.220
It's a single pane of
glass that gives you

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.510
real visibility, administrative
visibility, monitoring

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:44.450
and logging visibility,
across workloads

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.770
running in Google Cloud--

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.780
GCP, Google Cloud Platform--

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.600
as well as on prem
in your data center.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.460
You're able to
orchestrate and manage

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.210
those workloads, those
containers, just the same

00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:00.655
whether it's on prem
or in the Cloud.

00:06:00.655 --> 00:06:02.030
The operating
model is consistent

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.780
and I think that's important.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:04.785
It's really important.

00:06:04.785 --> 00:06:06.410
There's a lot of
variability in the way

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.900
that Kubernetes is used.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.230
And there are some
ways that are maybe

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.080
attractive from one
perspective or another.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.650
What we've done, we think,
is we've put a lot of effort

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.740
into the UI and the UX,
the user experience,

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:26.360
for how container orchestration
should look inside

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.210
of Google Cloud.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.130
And now that's available
on prem as well.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:38.170
But the operations is really
at the heart of it, right?

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.130
Google's site reliability
engineers, SREs,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.820
are absolutely
world class when it

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:48.850
comes to taking care of your
technology inside of our data

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.530
centers.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.720
But now we're using
those same tools

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.530
to install and configure and
automate and update, maintain,

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:01.270
and deploy that on prem the same
way we do that in Google Cloud.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.980
The environments are consistent.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.720
And consistency
is super important

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.270
when you're programming
against APIs.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.190
Knowing that those
APIs are going

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.070
to be the same in one
place as well as another.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.440
That those operating
system images

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:18.260
are available both hosted
in the Cloud and on prem,

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.770
updated, maintained, secured.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.080
And that the same components
are available in one

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.300
place and another.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.180
Now, the Google Cloud
is quite rich in terms

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.540
of how many different features
and plug-ins there are

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.890
and how tightly
those are coupled.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.300
And over time we're
working to bring as many

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:42.317
of those as possible
on prem as well.

00:07:42.317 --> 00:07:43.900
We're starting with
Kubernetes itself,

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:47.450
the core orchestration
of containers itself.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.050
And then you can
imagine the other things

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.500
that might make sense to
run alongside that inside

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.160
of your data center.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.750
But we go beyond that.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:01.150
So there's automated
operations, but there's also

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.320
management and help with that.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:11.760
And so we're offering full and
partially managed Kubernetes.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.970
Where the real magic is,
is in the automation,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.000
in the intelligence that we've
built over time as to what

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.940
it takes to keep a
node up and healthy,

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.260
running, predictive
when that node is not

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.630
going to be healthy at
some point in the future

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.628
so that we can take
corrective action.

00:08:30.628 --> 00:08:32.669
One of the most beautiful
things about Kubernetes

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.159
from a technology standpoint
is that it was designed

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.042
with this in mind, right?

00:08:37.042 --> 00:08:39.000
I've been around Linux
and open source software

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.630
for a couple of decades
and the number of projects

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.970
that add this sort
of health monitoring

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.670
after the fact, that's
almost the de facto standard.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:50.130
Kubernetes is sort of
the exception in that it

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.950
was built so that updates
and upgrades could

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:56.680
happen with zero downtime.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.130
It's quite an elegant
concept but it's

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.050
really simple at its core.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.150
Whenever we want
to upgrade a node,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.570
we take that node out of
the pool of eligible nodes

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.770
for workloads to be scheduled
to it so that no new workloads

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:10.680
show up on that node.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.150
We then drain that node of those
workloads, move them elsewhere.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.130
Then we can take that node
offline, do an upgrade.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.310
And then in some cases
we just, we kill the node

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.780
and we start a new node.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.180
All of that's built directly
into Kubernetes itself

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.210
and has been from day one.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.570
So from Cluster
Health, Auto Repair,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:34.740
these are services that
Google provides to GKE

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:40.150
and we're bringing to
you GKE On-Prem as well.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:44.860
OK, so that's the first
section of the talk.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.960
As the biggest
announcement, I think,

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.960
here this week GKE On-Prem
is an absolute game

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.530
changer for enterprise IT.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:57.976
The second section of this talk
is really the customer empathy

00:09:57.976 --> 00:09:59.020
section of the talk.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:02.920
Here, I think we've done
a lot of work and research

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.000
to understand why
hybrid cloud actually

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:11.140
is important to the enterprise
CIO, to the IT department,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:16.270
to the director of a data center
who has real needs-- real needs

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.940
to run some workloads
on prem and just can't

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:20.320
run those in the cloud.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.630
And so I've got roughly
10 use cases, use cases

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.420
that we've learned,
we've studied,

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:33.380
for why and when and where
hybrid cloud actually

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:33.950
makes sense.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.780
And those 10 separate
use cases sort of

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:38.000
fit into one of
these six buckets.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.410
I know I said 10, this is six.

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:44.330
We have six buckets and each
of the next 10 sort of apply.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:48.620
So for these next 10 slides,
I'd like just a little bit

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.690
of audience participation.

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:53.690
I'm going to show 10
different places where

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:58.584
we think hybrid cloud actually
matters and makes sense and is

00:10:58.584 --> 00:10:59.750
a requirement in some cases.

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.930
So if one any one of
these next 10 slides--

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:05.180
and you can vote
multiple times--

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.949
actually sings to you,
speaks to your heart,

00:11:07.949 --> 00:11:08.990
just give me a thumbs up.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:12.650
And that will help
us, especially some

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:16.190
of the other product managers
who are here in the audience,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.080
help us just
understand the impact

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.560
that some of these
approaches actually

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.049
have in your businesses.

00:11:23.049 --> 00:11:24.090
All right, so here we go.

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:25.048
So let's just try this.

00:11:25.048 --> 00:11:25.965
Thumbs up?

00:11:25.965 --> 00:11:26.840
All right, all right.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:27.881
That's all we have to do.

00:11:27.881 --> 00:11:28.580
Simple, right?

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:29.490
You don't have to stand up.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:30.781
You don't have to say anything.

00:11:30.781 --> 00:11:32.060
Just a simple thumbs up.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.780
OK, so one example of
a hybrid environment

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.940
that I've got personal
experience with

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.450
is working in the banking
and fin tech industry

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.030
where some workloads
have to run next

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.440
to a stock exchange, period.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.910
It just, laws of
physics, the wiring,

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:51.260
the copper between the fiber
between the data center

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:55.250
and the stock exchange,
the length of that matters.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:58.280
And a matter of a
few hundred meters

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:01.650
makes a difference from
a latency perspective.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.410
There are workload,
some workloads,

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.410
that need these
nanosecond latencies.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:08.500
This is very much event
driven processing.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.970
Something happens, and then
something else needs to happen.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.060
It needs to happen
very close to where

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.887
that first event happened.

00:12:16.887 --> 00:12:17.720
And it's real money.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:20.720
There's real money involved
when those latencies go

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:25.190
beyond a certain tolerance.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.720
There are in many cases very
specialized hardware involved,

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.600
hardware that's not available
in the public cloud.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.420
Some of this hardware
is custom designed

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:41.390
ASICs or very particular
field programmable hardware.

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:43.460
And highly
proprietary algorithms

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.920
that, for better or worse,
for one reason or another,

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:50.750
just will not move outside
of an on prem environment.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:53.840
Now, all of that said,
there are complements

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.720
to that on-prem workload that
make a lot of sense in a Google

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:58.220
Cloud.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:02.240
There are jobs that run
nightly as a batch job.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.580
In many cases we're
talking about models

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.010
that can be trained, and
are trained, and improve--

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.170
incrementally improve--
every single night,

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:11.512
but harness the
public cloud to do so.

00:13:11.512 --> 00:13:13.220
Those are not time
sensitive just as long

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.380
as it takes place once a night
or once a week or once a month

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.760
and integrates all of
the transactions that

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:23.600
have happened in the previous
day or week or month.

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:25.430
And that model
improves over time.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.130
And that can be run at
a much lower expense,

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:31.350
or perhaps more efficiently,
in the public cloud,

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:33.440
in Google Cloud.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:38.390
In many cases, those algorithms
are developed and tested

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:39.780
in the public cloud.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.350
But they end up running
locally next to the stock

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.730
exchange, or stock exchanges.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.910
And then from an event
driven standpoint,

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.330
Cloud Pub/Sub is an
excellent Google technology

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:53.150
that allows you to create
publications and subscriptions

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.480
and take certain actions
when this happens.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:59.760
All right, to anyone in
the room, does this one--

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:00.530
a few thumbs up.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.780
OK, good.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:07.230
So let's go from the
suits and pinstripes

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:10.910
to maybe what these
stockbrokers are

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:16.010
doing at night, which is
playing Counter Strike at home

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:16.530
at night.

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:18.350
So multiplayer online gaming--

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:22.100
increasingly so, and I've worked
with a number of companies

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:25.730
that deal with massive
multiplayer online gaming--

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:28.670
increasingly so,
the online games

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:32.000
where you're interfacing with
another player, your avatar

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:34.250
that you see on your
phone or on your screen,

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:38.327
is actually a container
running somewhere in the world.

00:14:38.327 --> 00:14:40.160
And the people that
you're interacting with,

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.165
that you're collaborating
with to build a city or you're

00:14:43.165 --> 00:14:45.290
shooting up because, you
know, they're the bad guys

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.090
and you're the
good guys, they're

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:49.984
actually a container
running, ideally,

00:14:49.984 --> 00:14:51.650
in the same data
center and perhaps even

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:52.858
on the same physical machine.

00:14:52.858 --> 00:14:55.790
And it ends up being a container
shooting at another container

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.190
where those things are
all happening inside

00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:59.300
of a data center.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:00.935
And it's happening like this.

00:15:00.935 --> 00:15:03.140
[SNAPPING FINGERS] And all
you're seeing, ultimately

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:06.240
on your screen, is a physical
rendering of what happens.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:07.760
And that's becoming
more and more

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.030
the standard as we play
games on little bitty devices

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:14.090
like this that have
very minuscule CPU

00:15:14.090 --> 00:15:16.396
and GPU to handle all
of that processing.

00:15:16.396 --> 00:15:18.020
And you're basically
watching a YouTube

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:21.530
video of yourself playing a
game, is what it ends up being.

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:25.040
And all of that happens
inside of a data center.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:27.800
Now, sometimes those
happen in public clouds.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:30.450
And Google's got
an excellent set

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:36.060
of customers running gaming
hosts inside of Google Cloud.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.860
But in some cases, those
require on prem resources.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.770
I've seen a few that
need bare metal--

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:45.780
no virtualization, bare metal.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:51.120
That tends to be
a requirement when

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.570
latencies are
extremely low and there

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:58.500
is no concern for
multi-tenancy, right?

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.780
And ultimately
these are apps that

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.970
are containerized
and running inside

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.670
of what amounts to a Kubernetes
or some sort of container

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:07.110
scheduling algorithm.

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:09.630
Now, what happens
in the public cloud?

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:10.980
What happens in Google Cloud?

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:13.090
Well, often there is a
global control plane.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.250
There's consolidated
identity and billing.

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:19.980
That's the first point of
entry to either the web

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.450
interface or the app interface.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:25.560
And then traffic gets directed
to separate geo located,

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.830
perhaps, environments.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:33.886
So from stock exchange to
multiplayer online gaming.

00:16:33.886 --> 00:16:35.760
Does anyone have a
business need around this?

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.340
Or is this just like fun?

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:40.230
OK, cool, bit of both, great.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:44.277
So, a third case is the
telco base station, OK?

00:16:44.277 --> 00:16:46.110
I've spent a lot of
time working with telco,

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.480
and I've had the privilege
to actually visit

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.040
a couple of telco base stations.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.647
Has anyone ever been inside
of a telco base station,

00:16:52.647 --> 00:16:53.730
underneath the cell tower?

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.559
There, all right, good.

00:16:55.559 --> 00:16:56.850
You can tell us what it's like.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:59.520
But the ones that I've seen,
especially the new ones

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:02.940
that are being built to
handle 5G connectivity,

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.069
involve multiple
racks of servers.

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:07.319
Typically they're
caged off in such a way

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:09.839
that Sprint has one cage,
and Verizon has another cage,

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:12.230
and AT&amp;T has a third cage.

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:13.859
And inside of each
of those cages

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:15.420
there's a rack of hardware.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.960
In a small cell tower
it's a half rack.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:21.210
In some of the big ones
it's multiple racks

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:24.839
of real, physical
servers cranking

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:29.370
the data that's going to your
phone and from your phone.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:31.230
And these are local
applications that

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.690
run inside of each cell tower.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:37.980
There are content distribution
networks, caching of data.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.710
You know, we're watching
more videos on our phones.

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:42.750
And I certainly enjoy
watching the World Cup.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.910
I did a lot of
that from my phone.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:48.330
Much of that data was being
cached at the local cell

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:51.270
tower, when you're watching
that over a 4G connection

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:52.630
for instance.

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:56.910
And these are largely
third party applications,

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.190
not developed by the telco,
applications that they

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.730
buy to handle the
routing of SMS messages

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:06.600
and the playing of
content and simple stuff

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:09.190
like making phone calls, right?

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.760
And there is a massive
amount of computing power

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:12.970
necessary to do that.

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:15.420
And it has to be done
at the cell tower.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:17.504
It has to happen
at the cell tower.

00:18:17.504 --> 00:18:19.170
Now what's really
interesting about this

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.086
is that this is a case
where there's typically

00:18:21.086 --> 00:18:25.110
lots of computing power and
a huge pipe of data flowing

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:30.380
from that cell tower back to
the data center or to the cloud.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.480
And so this is
definitely a case where

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:38.640
we've seen the vendors in
this space wanting Kubernetes,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.440
or something like
Kubernetes, to handle

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:43.680
the orchestration of those
many and varied apps, apps that

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.110
are being developed and
released and patched

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:51.284
and updated at cycles that
are asynchronous with the rest

00:18:51.284 --> 00:18:53.700
of the development cycles, and
certainly with one another,

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:54.200
right?

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:55.800
You've got vendors
in that cell tower

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.910
from lots of
different companies.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.970
Now, what happens
inside of Google Cloud

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:06.300
could be the control
plane, again.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.330
Certainly the handling of the
remote management and updates.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.800
When you've got
tens of thousands

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:13.695
of these cell towers
rolling out updates,

00:19:13.695 --> 00:19:17.760
it requires a very centralized
management control plane.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.740
And then consolidating
the logging and analytics,

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:23.520
being able to audit those,
search those, detect failures,

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.640
train models on when some
hardware is about to fail

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:30.270
and you have to roll a truck out
there to replace some hardware.

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:32.610
All right, so telco
industry-- anyone?

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:34.050
Has anyone seen this?

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:36.330
Yeah, exactly.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:38.210
This need for hybrid computing.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:40.470
So video transcoding.

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.520
These are in no
particular order,

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:45.030
but video transcoding
is a little bit

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:48.840
related to that
multiplayer online gaming

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.420
situation in that it's in
the media and entertainment

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:52.480
industry.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:57.840
But we've seen a ton of video
transcoding algorithms being

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.280
implemented on
top of Kubernetes.

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:02.970
It's a perfect platform
for dividing up

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:05.460
massive jobs of transcoding.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.800
Typically, what
happens when some video

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.300
needs to be produced
and delivered

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:10.620
to all of our devices--

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.890
you know, we've got many
and varied devices just here

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:15.870
in this room from our
phones to our laptops

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:18.960
to iPads or tablets.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.620
Each of those has a
different resolution.

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.840
And in many cases, the
video is transcode it

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.380
for literally every
different resolution

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.950
that it might need to
be transmit to just so

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:33.680
that it can be done as
efficiently and optimized

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.400
as well as possible for
your particular environment.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.740
Now, there's the fun and
exciting things like movies

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:46.070
in 3D and 4K and
working with some

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:50.512
of the interesting video
production houses in Hollywood.

00:20:50.512 --> 00:20:52.970
And then there's the really
boring stuff, which is actually

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:54.780
most of the video in the world.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:57.260
And it's the
security cameras that

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:03.290
are around every public place,
every shopping mall, hotel.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:07.610
Much of that video transcoding
takes place first on prem.

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:09.964
And then some of it is
archived to the cloud.

00:21:09.964 --> 00:21:11.630
Samples of it gets
archived to the cloud

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:13.970
or there's cold storage
being stored in the cloud.

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:16.400
But it often takes a
local transcoding job

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.335
to handle the massive
amount of data.

00:21:19.335 --> 00:21:20.960
So again, this is
just laws of physics.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.640
The data can't transfer fast
enough to the public cloud.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:27.110
Some of that needs to be
done in local and on prem.

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:30.230
But once it gets
to Google Cloud,

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:35.750
some of the vision ML APIs
that are being developed inside

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:38.780
of Google Cloud are
absolutely amazing.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:40.580
If you've used
Google Photos, I've

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.910
been blown away by going
through 20 years of photos

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:45.530
that I've uploaded
to Google Photos.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:51.680
And I was looking for this
picture of a watermelon

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:54.830
that in college we
blew up because it's

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:59.569
fun to blow up things when
you're in college in Texas.

00:21:59.569 --> 00:22:00.860
And so we blew up a watermelon.

00:22:00.860 --> 00:22:03.068
And we were laughing about
this, me and some buddies,

00:22:03.068 --> 00:22:04.970
at our union some time ago.

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:07.095
And I just wondered,
can I find that picture?

00:22:07.095 --> 00:22:08.720
And sure enough, I
typed in watermelon.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.330
I found about two dozen
watermelon pictures

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:12.320
over the years.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:13.840
And it was amazing.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.560
One was like a dress
my daughter was

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:19.229
wearing with little
watermelon cartoons on it.

00:22:19.229 --> 00:22:21.395
And Google Photos figured
out that was a watermelon.

00:22:21.395 --> 00:22:23.561
And sure enough, we found
the watermelon we blew up.

00:22:23.561 --> 00:22:25.920
And that was a fun time.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.490
But the fact that once you
get that data into the cloud

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:31.490
and you can run those
algorithms, video transcoding

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:33.950
is extremely powerful
when coupling on prem

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:35.700
with public cloud.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:38.390
So video transcoding, has
anyone had video transcoding

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:41.000
jobs that they've
needed to run on prem?

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.580
Yeah.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:48.940
So, retail-- I think I've met
more retail customers this week

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:50.980
than maybe ever in my career.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.630
And I've dealt with
some big retailers.

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:55.870
But this particular
week has been

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:59.110
an extremely enlightening
and interesting set

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:02.800
of conversations around
retail computing.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:08.750
And the retail industry, as
much as we buy things online

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.980
and they get delivered
to our house,

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:17.080
there still is this massive,
massive brick and mortar

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:18.190
retail industry.

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:20.650
And it's not going anywhere.

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.170
And in fact, that
retail industry

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:28.160
is investing heavily in clusters
of servers and computers

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.174
there, on prem, inside
of a shopping center.

00:23:30.174 --> 00:23:31.840
Almost every shopping
center, whether it

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:35.450
be a mall or a strip mall
or just a set of stores,

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:38.860
shares a little bit of
compute typically provided

00:23:38.860 --> 00:23:45.490
by the landlord or the owner
of that retail who usually owns

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:48.150
a few of those and has a very
similar software and hardware

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:50.800
setup spread across
those different things.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:52.810
And whether it's a
giant big box retailer

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:57.520
or it's a small chain
of stores, there's

00:23:57.520 --> 00:24:01.261
consistency in the hardware and
software that gets rolled out.

00:24:01.261 --> 00:24:03.760
So there's typically, whether
you realize it or not, there's

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.340
typically a cluster of servers
in the back of the shopping

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.799
center that handles a
bunch of local apps.

00:24:08.799 --> 00:24:10.840
And we've already talked
about video transcoding.

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:15.700
But there's about a
dozen cameras per--

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:16.750
what is the number?

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:20.830
Per 1,000 square feet
to cover that much area.

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:24.610
And there's a reel of
tape-- and it's not

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:29.140
tape-- a reel of hard drives
storing those recordings

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:31.930
locally so that when
there is a problem

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:35.210
and they need to give off that
data to local law enforcement,

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:36.010
they can do so.

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:36.940
And that's all local.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:37.960
That hasn't been transferred.

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:40.330
And then there's cold storage
that happens in the cloud

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.490
and in archive.

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:44.800
And then there's
inventory management.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:49.360
Some building automation,
the HVAC or the lighting,

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:51.250
being able to handle
and control that.

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:55.120
In some cases, there are city
regulations that some of that

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:57.280
needs to happen and be
able to happen locally

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:59.710
in the case of an
emergency and has

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.570
to have a fallback
plan when it can't

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.784
connect to an internet
connected service.

00:25:03.784 --> 00:25:05.450
And of course, the
point of sale system.

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:08.620
The heart of every business
is a point of sale system.

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:10.420
Typically your credit
card transactions

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:11.660
are logged locally.

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:16.300
They're batch sent to
the payment processor.

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:18.400
But there's a backup kept
for some amount of time

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:20.110
just in case something happens.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:22.360
You've probably seen that
credit card transaction

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:26.020
that didn't show up for a
few weeks or a month or two,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:27.310
but then showed back up.

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:29.770
And it was because someone
had to go back in and check

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:31.510
an audit log, found
something missing,

00:25:31.510 --> 00:25:35.440
and was able to pull that off
of a second copy or a local copy

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:36.370
perhaps.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:40.600
Now all that said, Google
Cloud offers a real compliment

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:47.470
to this retail cluster, whether
that's a global web presence,

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:51.100
being able to direct that
traffic geographically

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:55.530
to the best possible
location or server,

00:25:55.530 --> 00:26:00.610
certainly cloud storage for the
video and those transactions,

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:03.730
the AutoML, and the insights,
the buying patterns,

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:06.100
the user buying patterns.

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:08.200
There's been a number of
good sessions this week

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.450
about the retail industry.

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:12.360
So retail, sorry.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:15.530
Anyone here from-- or, sorry,
let me not ask anyone here

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:16.030
from retail.

00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:18.910
Does this use case speak
to some of your needs?

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:21.160
Yep.

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:23.050
Smart buildings and
smart cities-- you know,

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:28.300
one of the biggest places I
think we will see in this next

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:33.110
decade a real market improvement
in our life is through

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:37.030
automation, safety,
improvements--

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:39.340
technology driven
improvements-- in the way

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:42.080
we live and interact
with one another.

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:44.822
And that can be in both rural
environments as well as highly

00:26:44.822 --> 00:26:46.030
dense environments like this.

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:47.821
And this really starts
with smart buildings

00:26:47.821 --> 00:26:48.550
and smart cities.

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:52.845
So just about everything that
I said about a retail shopping

00:26:52.845 --> 00:26:54.970
center that has a rack of
servers doing things that

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:58.540
have to be done locally, as
well as most of what I said

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.300
about a telco base station with
some applications that need

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:04.900
to run locally, if you go
to any of these high rise

00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:07.840
modern skyscrapers
there's a rack

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:10.420
of servers in the basement
doing building automation

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:12.730
and certain transactions
that have to happen

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:15.130
within that building itself.

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:17.560
And I've said security I
don't know how many times now,

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:22.920
but it ends up being quite a
bit of security feeds, HVAC,

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.480
and just running the operations
of the building itself.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:29.470
Now the smart part of smart
buildings and smart cities

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:33.640
is using AI and ML to
train models and improve

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:40.660
the performance and operation
of some of those aspects

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:43.420
that we come to depend on from
a technology and infrastructure

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:44.470
perspective.

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:48.160
Training those in the cloud and
sending the models, the trained

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:51.890
models, back down to the
clusters to implement

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:55.530
is really an
interesting handshake.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:57.280
So smart buildings,
smart cities, this one

00:27:57.280 --> 00:27:58.780
might be a little
bit on the fringe.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:01.300
But does anyone have any
experience in this space

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.400
and see hybrid computing?

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:07.060
Yeah, that's good.

00:28:07.060 --> 00:28:10.780
So data provenance is one
that I think we've all

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:12.010
experienced this year.

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:15.370
Anyone who's had anything
to do with GDPR, and then

00:28:15.370 --> 00:28:19.810
prior to GDPR or any of
the other personal privacy

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:21.520
protection--

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:23.380
either legislation
or regulations

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:25.900
and in some cases
just our own processes

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.290
that we have in the enterprise
around where data can be,

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:31.420
how long it can
live, how it needs

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:32.890
to be encrypted and treated.

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:36.460
And in some cases there are
simply legal obligations,

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.770
there are compliance
requirements,

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.420
around where that data lives.

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:46.750
And no matter how secure
and encrypted at all levels

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:50.980
Google Cloud actually is,
there are some processes

00:28:50.980 --> 00:28:54.940
that just require some data to
be stored locally and on prem.

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:59.570
And we will continue
to improve the security

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:02.750
and the compliances and
the list of compliances--

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:05.500
it's actually quite an
impressive list at this link

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:06.130
here--

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:10.604
of compliance audits that
Google Cloud has passed.

00:29:10.604 --> 00:29:11.770
We'll continue growing that.

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:14.390
But there is some data that
for one reason or another,

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:16.120
it needs to live somewhere else.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:18.020
And perhaps it's
inside of a country.

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:20.380
In some cases it may just be
for a liability limitation.

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:22.540
There's an insurance
policy that a company might

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:27.010
have where their insurance
policy is different depending

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:29.590
on where their data
is stored, or that's

00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:32.770
what their auditors
have told them.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:34.930
Now that said, inside
of Google Cloud

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:38.250
we do have a really rich
security feature set.

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:40.960
Your data is encrypted at every
single level all the way down

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:43.300
the stack of virtual machines.

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:47.470
You're booting in Trusted
Boot environments.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:49.780
And then there are extremely
fine grained identity

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:50.710
and access controls.

00:29:50.710 --> 00:29:56.550
Our back access controls around
that you have control of as to

00:29:56.550 --> 00:30:01.060
in your organization who can
access given buckets of data.

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:03.820
So from that
perspective, this is

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:06.910
a case where some data
lives in some places

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:08.770
and some data is
replicated elsewhere.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:11.320
Some data is encrypted
with a set of keys

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:14.890
that stores the data, but it's
protected unless those keys are

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:16.400
available.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:21.260
So from a data
provenance perspective,

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:24.820
this is another driver
for some hybrid use cases.

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:28.150
Does this resonate with anyone?

00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:32.590
Wow, that's a
popular one for sure.

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:35.160
So then we go toward
lift and shift.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:38.440
Lift and shift being maybe
some traditional workloads

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:42.370
that have long lived in
an enterprise data center,

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:44.020
but for one reason
or another you'd

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:46.660
like to move them to
the cloud, possibly even

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:52.180
in the other direction in that
it can really go either way.

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:55.990
I've seen, and I've worked with,
a couple of large customers

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:59.140
moving virtual
machines to the cloud.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:02.350
Google acquired a company
called Velostrata.

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:07.430
And I'm the product manager
associated with Velostrata

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:11.740
as we're working to integrate
Velostrata a bit tighter

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:12.574
into Google Cloud.

00:31:12.574 --> 00:31:13.990
But Velostrata has
some technology

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:17.560
that will take a VMware
virtual machine image

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:21.010
and live stream that
image to Google Compute

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:23.560
Engine without
downtime and actually

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:28.590
move that VMware image into
a VM in the public cloud,

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:30.400
in Google's cloud.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:32.430
And it's really
quite impressive.

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:33.310
It's a great demo.

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:36.640
And it's something that we've
helped organizations move

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.130
thousands of legacy
applications running

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:41.440
in VMware inside of
their data center

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:45.520
into a hosted cloud environment.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:49.150
I've certainly been in a data
center with old dusty machines

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:51.790
that no one's quite
sure how it's still

00:31:51.790 --> 00:31:53.230
working because,
you know, the fan

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:56.050
makes a noise about
60 times a second.

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:58.220
And at any point
it could fall over.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:01.110
And boy, that's just
going to be a bad day.

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:03.670
And so moving some of those
workloads into the cloud

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:06.566
tends to be a priority.

00:32:06.566 --> 00:32:08.440
And in some cases, this
is what people really

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:09.814
mean when they
mean hybrid cloud,

00:32:09.814 --> 00:32:13.630
being able to just move a
good old VM from one place

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:14.260
to another.

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:16.551
And we're seeing more and
more of that with Kubernetes.

00:32:16.551 --> 00:32:19.870
As these applications
are lifted and shifted

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.450
they're actually
moved and improved.

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:27.450
They're modernized as they
actually move into a cloud.

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:31.030
And there are some advantages
to be had by moving them

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:32.830
into a container.

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:35.980
Not having to worry about
kernel updates, for instance.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.740
And that the real point is that
most legacy software actually

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:39.650
predates the cloud.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:42.800
That's sort of the definition of
legacy at this juncture in time

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:44.280
anyway.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:48.740
And from that perspective,
some of those workloads

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.760
can't or won't or
just are not well

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:54.520
suited to moving
to the Google Cloud

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:56.580
for one reason or another.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:33:01.590
And that might be impetus
for, in some cases,

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:05.540
using both on prem for some
workloads and public cloud

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:08.010
for other workloads.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:09.650
So from a lift and
shift perspective,

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:13.930
is this an initiative with
within anyone's organization?

00:33:13.930 --> 00:33:18.750
Yeah, we see that quite a bit.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:19.760
So this is a fun one.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:24.190
And I actually know of a
really interesting young man.

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:27.200
He's interned for me a
couple of different times.

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:32.480
But in his last internship he
had this really interesting

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:36.020
job, which was to create a
calculator that would determine

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:38.480
for a given job--

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:40.100
he was working
for a company that

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:43.640
did a lot of large,
large scale AI ML job,

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:46.070
jobs that cost tens of
thousands of dollars

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:47.570
to run just one time.

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:49.940
Just running this job one
time it was tens of thousands

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:51.090
of dollars.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:54.470
And his job as an intern was
to figure out the cheapest way

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:57.720
to run that job,
meaning which cloud,

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:02.840
whether to use GPUs or TPUs,
how big the instances should be.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:08.239
And that tool saved
this company maybe 20%,

00:34:08.239 --> 00:34:11.179
maybe $2,000,
$3,000, for each job

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:14.810
if it could find the
cheapest right way to run it.

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:21.420
Now their organization
was entirely public cloud,

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:23.120
but I've seen this
model actually

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:25.010
work where sometimes
it makes more sense

00:34:25.010 --> 00:34:29.330
to simply run a job on prem
for one reason or another.

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:31.219
In other cases it makes
more economic sense

00:34:31.219 --> 00:34:34.830
to run that in Google
Cloud, for instance.

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:38.719
And so you get this sort
of decision tree of when

00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:41.179
to do which and it's
actually quite simple--

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:42.980
wherever it's
cheaper, wherever it's

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:45.920
less expensive or costs less.

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:48.120
And I call that the
economic arbitrage.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:49.969
It's like we're going
to arbitrage this job

00:34:49.969 --> 00:34:53.150
and run it where
it's most efficient.

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:54.800
Does anyone have to
make this decision,

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:58.830
or has an algorithm to make
this decision for them?

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:00.660
Yeah.

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:02.070
It's a real driver for business.

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:06.530
And you know, it can show
you where your efficiencies

00:35:06.530 --> 00:35:08.250
are available.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.972
So this is kind of the classic
dev test CI CD into prod.

00:35:11.972 --> 00:35:13.430
I've worked with
organizations that

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:15.388
do all of their development
in the public cloud

00:35:15.388 --> 00:35:19.002
but then run their
production servers on prem.

00:35:19.002 --> 00:35:20.460
And I've seen
exactly the opposite.

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:23.510
We do all of our development
on our laptops, on our Macs,

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:26.030
on our Linux machines.

00:35:26.030 --> 00:35:30.530
But then we run and host
our production servers

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:32.200
in the cloud.

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:33.200
And so, you know what?

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:36.500
We're not prescriptive about
that from this perspective.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.360
I'm certainly not
prescriptive about it.

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:40.400
Whatever makes sense.

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:42.290
Bringing GKE on
prem, for instance,

00:35:42.290 --> 00:35:44.080
gives you the
ability to do either.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.820
You can use the on prem
cluster for your dev and test

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:48.470
and GKE for production.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:50.340
I certainly think that
makes a lot of sense.

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:52.715
But you could just as easily
do it in the other direction

00:35:52.715 --> 00:35:55.920
and send your load balancers
and your traffic elsewhere.

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:58.670
And so I think this is one
of the classic hybrid models,

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:02.732
using a bit of public
cloud, a bit of on prem.

00:36:02.732 --> 00:36:04.440
I'm guessing a few
people have experience

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:06.662
with doing this for sure.

00:36:06.662 --> 00:36:09.260
Yeah.

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:12.080
And then finally,
the last one is--

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:14.570
and I've seen this
now from a number

00:36:14.570 --> 00:36:16.820
of banks in some regulated
industries that are

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:19.350
deemed just too big to fail--

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:21.410
that they're actually
spreading workloads

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:26.900
across multiple clouds,
where running a Google Cloud

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:27.680
is great.

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:30.760
And then having
another plan actually

00:36:30.760 --> 00:36:36.690
is required by industry
regulations just in case.

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:40.520
And so there's a lot of
reasons that could go here.

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:46.940
But I think this is a
different type of hybrid cloud.

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:49.850
This one isn't necessarily
on prem to Google Cloud.

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:51.140
It certainly can be.

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:55.190
But it could be from other
clouds to Google Cloud.

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:58.786
So does this one hit
home with anyone?

00:36:58.786 --> 00:36:59.700
Yeah.

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:02.420
OK, so that's our 10 use cases.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:03.710
I've studied each of these.

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:06.350
And we've put together,
from a product and strategy

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:10.850
team, put together quite
a number of critical user

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:12.320
journeys through each of these.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:17.150
And we are working from
a hybrid cloud computing

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:19.340
standpoint with these in mind.

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:23.060
So maybe at the end
I'll ask if there's--

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:24.080
I'll just ask right now.

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:25.496
I've got just a
couple of minutes.

00:37:25.496 --> 00:37:28.850
Is there any major use
case that I've missed here?

00:37:28.850 --> 00:37:32.310
Or do these pretty
much cover it?

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:32.810
Yes.

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:34.695
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:34.695 --> 00:37:36.195
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Research Institute?

00:37:36.195 --> 00:37:37.440
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:45.280
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:46.830
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
GPU hardware, yep.

00:37:46.830 --> 00:37:50.030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:52.510
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
that's a great example.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:54.260
They've got a lot of
hardware that they've

00:37:54.260 --> 00:37:57.122
invested in, GPU hardware
in a research institute.

00:37:57.122 --> 00:37:58.580
And it's sort of
there and you want

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:00.560
to use it because it's there.

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:03.080
But you want to take advantage
of newer GPUs, perhaps newer

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:06.680
hardware, and the
burstability of public cloud.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.100
Good, thank you.

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:11.630
OK, so the third
point to make here

00:38:11.630 --> 00:38:14.390
is that we really do
have a world class cloud.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:16.940
I hope that you've
enjoyed your week here.

00:38:16.940 --> 00:38:19.550
I think we're
starting to wrap up.

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:22.700
I found this an amazing
three day exhibition

00:38:22.700 --> 00:38:27.020
of just innovation
and inspiration

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:30.470
of how much work my
colleagues, my engineers,

00:38:30.470 --> 00:38:32.840
put into what they do every day.

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:34.520
I've learned about
corners of Google

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:36.980
that I didn't know existed.

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:41.000
Some of the announcements
were new to me as to you here.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:44.690
Hopefully, you've been
educated a little bit.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:49.730
But I think hopefully the
previous section shows you

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:52.430
that we really
know, we've really

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:55.550
come to understand-- we're
coming to understand what

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:57.680
hybrid cloud is by
classifying this,

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:02.250
understanding these use cases,
having this sort of feedback.

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:04.700
But I think that the inverse
is actually true as well.

00:39:04.700 --> 00:39:08.570
I think hybrid actually knows
Google, and actually uses

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:10.880
Google, in a lot
of different ways.

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:16.010
If we start with the last 10,
15 years of Google products,

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:20.210
Enterprise products,
it goes from G Suite,

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:22.910
from using Google Docs
and maybe Hangouts

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:26.570
or Mail calendaring
inside of the enterprise,

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:29.940
while the enterprise still
has its own IT data center.

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:33.300
This is a piece of
IT that used to be

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:38.210
in servers stored locally,
storage maybe for documents.

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.340
Who remembers emailing
documents around?

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.760
Boy what a miserable time it
was to work in this industry

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:48.439
when everybody would get a
copy, their own copy, of a doc.

00:39:48.439 --> 00:39:50.480
And I mean, how did you
do collaborative editing?

00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:52.250
I couldn't live
without that today.

00:39:52.250 --> 00:39:54.900
It's like going back
to a typewriter.

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:56.240
The leap is almost the same.

00:39:56.240 --> 00:39:59.030
But now that's all
stored essentially

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:03.620
in Google's public cloud,
but secured and cordoned off.

00:40:03.620 --> 00:40:07.520
To Android Enterprise,
being able to use

00:40:07.520 --> 00:40:09.590
those same apps
securely on a phone,

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:13.400
managed by an IT
security organization--

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:15.800
your own IT security
organization who does that

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:18.810
through Google's technology.

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:23.790
And then it's really coming
to Google Cloud, as well.

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:27.980
The enterprise using,
leveraging, Google Cloud.

00:40:27.980 --> 00:40:31.310
And I'm here to really just
talk about Kubernetes engine.

00:40:31.310 --> 00:40:34.700
But there's so many of these
products that are actually

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:39.980
quite applicable to the
enterprise and bridge that gap.

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:42.560
And that's the point here.

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:47.060
We're working hard to bridge
the gap between your data center

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:48.950
and Google's services.

00:40:48.950 --> 00:40:50.720
And I say services
and not servers

00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:54.560
because we really are looking
at this from a services

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:55.350
perspective.

00:40:55.350 --> 00:40:58.130
We produce services that
you can consume APIs

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:00.470
that you can talk
to, applications

00:41:00.470 --> 00:41:05.570
but those applications end up
being Google services in fact.

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:08.100
And GKE On-Prem is
super special here.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:10.130
This is really the
first time we're

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:14.780
bringing Google's software into
the enterprise data center.

00:41:14.780 --> 00:41:17.420
The only other
example we can really

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:20.000
think of here where Google's
done something similar

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:21.980
is the Search Appliance,
which was actually

00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:24.960
a physical hardware appliance
that you installed in your rack

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:29.750
and indexed your data,
documents, websites,

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:31.231
on your internal internet.

00:41:31.231 --> 00:41:32.480
That was a physical appliance.

00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:33.840
This is different.

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:37.040
This is software,
Google software,

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:40.217
that runs inside of your
enterprise data center.

00:41:40.217 --> 00:41:41.300
And that's a game changer.

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:42.500
That's really different.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:49.140
I can't understate how important
that change actually is.

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:52.555
And so, wrapping this all
up and bringing it together,

00:41:52.555 --> 00:41:53.930
I think if you
were going to take

00:41:53.930 --> 00:41:56.180
a picture of one slide
this is the one that I

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:58.370
think says it all here.

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:02.600
If you really treat Google
Cloud as an extension

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:06.170
of your own data center,
just imagine how powerful

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:08.270
your data center ends up being.

00:42:08.270 --> 00:42:11.990
Its sort of the
analogy of your phone

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:13.400
that has access to the internet.

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:16.450
And suddenly this
tiny little thing

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:19.100
has access to basically
all of the information that

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:22.070
has ever been created in
the history of mankind.

00:42:22.070 --> 00:42:24.560
And this is your
interface to it.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:28.520
If your data center itself
is connected to Google Cloud

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.920
and using Google
Cloud technologies,

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:34.235
just imagine how powerful
that data center becomes.

